/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Jeremiah chapters 21-25
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("jeremiah", [
{n:21, name:"The Way of Life or Death", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 21:8",verse:"And unto this people thou shalt say, Thus saith the LORD; Behold, I set before you the way of life, and the way of death."}, facts:["Did you know Zedekiah sent two men, Pashur and the priest Zephaniah, to ask Jeremiah to pray, hoping God would repeat His old wonders against Babylon (Jeremiah 21:1-2)?","Did you know God's answer to the king's prayer request was the starkest choice in Scripture: 'I set before you the way of life, and the way of death' (Jeremiah 21:8)?","Did you know God said He Himself would fight against Jerusalem 'with an outstretched hand and with a strong arm,' the very arm that had once fought for them (Jeremiah 21:5)?"], questions:[
{q:"Who did King Zedekiah send to Jeremiah?", options:["Pashur the son of Melchiah, and Zephaniah the son of Maaseiah the priest","Hananiah the son of Azur, and Shemaiah","Gedaliah the son of Ahikam, and Johanan","Baruch the scribe, and Seraiah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:1",verse:"The word which came unto Jeremiah from the LORD, when king Zedekiah sent unto him Pashur the son of Melchiah, and Zephaniah the son of Maaseiah the priest, saying,", insight:"A king under siege sends for a prophet; trouble makes even stubborn hearts seek a word from God."},
{q:"What did the messengers ask Jeremiah to do?", options:["Enquire of the LORD for us","Bless the armies of Judah","Prophesy a quick victory","Offer a sacrifice for the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:2",verse:"Enquire, I pray thee, of the LORD for us; for Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon maketh war against us; if so be that the LORD will deal with us according to all his wondrous works, that he may go up from us.", insight:"They wanted prayer without repentance; asking God for wonders while ignoring His warnings is hollow asking."},
{q:"Which king of Babylon was making war against Judah?", options:["Nebuchadrezzar","Belshazzar","Evil-merodach","Nabopolassar"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:2",verse:"Enquire, I pray thee, of the LORD for us; for Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon maketh war against us; if so be that the LORD will deal with us according to all his wondrous works, that he may go up from us.", insight:"The name that haunted Judah; God would soon call this very king His servant, for heaven drafts its own instruments."},
{q:"What did God say He would do with Judah's weapons of war?", options:["Turn them back, and assemble them into the midst of the city","Melt them down at the temple","Give them new strength","Scatter them among the Chaldeans"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:4",verse:"Thus saith the LORD God of Israel; Behold, I will turn back the weapons of war that are in your hands, wherewith ye fight against the king of Babylon, and against the Chaldeans, which besiege you without the walls, and I will assemble them into the midst of this city.", insight:"Their own weapons would be gathered against them; fighting God turns your armor into your enemy."},
{q:"How did the LORD say He Himself would fight against them?", options:["With an outstretched hand and a strong arm, in anger, fury, and great wrath","With hailstones from heaven","With a plague of locusts alone","Through angels with drawn swords"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:5",verse:"And I myself will fight against you with an outstretched hand and with a strong arm, even in anger, and in fury, and in great wrath.", insight:"The same outstretched arm that brought them out of Egypt was now stretched against them; God's power is terrible when aimed at rebellion."},
{q:"What would smite both man and beast in the city?", options:["A great pestilence","A flood","Wild beasts","A famine of bread only"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:6",verse:"And I will smite the inhabitants of this city, both man and beast: they shall die of a great pestilence.", insight:"From prince to cattle, none were out of reach; judgment touches everything we lean on."},
{q:"Into whose hand would Zedekiah and the survivors be delivered?", options:["Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon","Pharaoh king of Egypt","The king of Assyria","The king of Moab"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:7",verse:"And afterward, saith the LORD, I will deliver Zedekiah king of Judah, and his servants, and the people, and such as are left in this city from the pestilence, from the sword, and from the famine, into the hand of Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon, and into the hand of their enemies, and into the hand of those that seek their life: and he shall smite them with the edge of the sword; he shall not spare them, neither have pity, nor have mercy.", insight:"No pity, no mercy, no sparing; the king who would not hear the prophet had to hear the conqueror."},
{q:"What two ways did God set before the people?", options:["The way of life, and the way of death","The way of the sea, and the way of the land","The way of peace, and the way of war","The way of the rich, and the way of the poor"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:8",verse:"And unto this people thou shalt say, Thus saith the LORD; Behold, I set before you the way of life, and the way of death.", insight:"Every soul still faces these two roads; God lays them bare and urges the better one."},
{q:"Who would live, according to Jeremiah's word?", options:["He that goeth out, and falleth to the Chaldeans","He that abideth in the city","He that fleeth to Egypt","He that hideth in the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:9",verse:"He that abideth in this city shall die by the sword, and by the famine, and by the pestilence: but he that goeth out, and falleth to the Chaldeans that besiege you, he shall live, and his life shall be unto him for a prey.", insight:"Surrender looked like death but meant life; sometimes living means laying down your arms."},
{q:"What awaited those who stayed in the city?", options:["Death by the sword, the famine, and the pestilence","Prosperity and peace","Carriage to Babylon as princes","Protection behind the walls"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:9",verse:"He that abideth in this city shall die by the sword, and by the famine, and by the pestilence: but he that goeth out, and falleth to the Chaldeans that besiege you, he shall live, and his life shall be unto him for a prey.", insight:"Three judgments named together; staying put in disobedience is not safety."},
{q:"What had the LORD set against Jerusalem?", options:["His face, for evil and not for good","His angels","A wall of fire","His covenant"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:10",verse:"For I have set my face against this city for evil, and not for good, saith the LORD: it shall be given into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he shall burn it with fire.", insight:"The face that once shone upon them was now set against them; favor lost becomes judgment gained."},
{q:"How would the king of Babylon take the city?", options:["He shall burn it with fire","He shall starve it seven years","He shall tear down the walls with engines","He shall poison the wells"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:10",verse:"For I have set my face against this city for evil, and not for good, saith the LORD: it shall be given into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he shall burn it with fire.", insight:"Fire would finish what sword began; God's sentence on the city was thorough."},
{q:"To whom was the warning about executing judgment addressed?", options:["The house of the king of Judah","The priests of the temple","The captains of the guard","The elders of the city"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:11",verse:"And touching the house of the king of Judah, say, Hear ye the word of the LORD;", insight:"Judgment begins at the king's house; leaders answer first."},
{q:"When were they to execute judgment?", options:["In the morning","At the new moon","Once a year","At midnight"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:12",verse:"O house of David, thus saith the LORD; Execute judgment in the morning, and deliver him that is spoiled out of the hand of the oppressor, lest my fury go out like fire, and burn that none can quench it, because of the evil of your doings.", insight:"In the morning means promptly, day by day; justice delayed is justice denied."},
{q:"Why was unquenchable fury warned of?", options:["Because of the evil of your doings","Because of their fathers' sins","Because they trusted Egypt","Because they neglected the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:12",verse:"O house of David, thus saith the LORD; Execute judgment in the morning, and deliver him that is spoiled out of the hand of the oppressor, lest my fury go out like fire, and burn that none can quench it, because of the evil of your doings.", insight:"Their own doings kindled the fire; no one can quench what his own sins lit."},
{q:"Who was called the inhabitant of the valley, and rock of the plain?", options:["Jerusalem, secure in her position","The Ammonites","The Philistines","The Edomites"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:13",verse:"Behold, I am against thee, O inhabitant of the valley, and rock of the plain, saith the LORD; which say, Who shall come down against us? or who shall enter into our habitations?", insight:"A city boasting of her natural defenses; geography is no shield when God is the invader."},
{q:"What question betrayed their false confidence?", options:["Who shall come down against us? or who shall enter into our habitations?","Who will help us in battle?","Who will feed us in the famine?","Who will open the gates?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:13",verse:"Behold, I am against thee, O inhabitant of the valley, and rock of the plain, saith the LORD; which say, Who shall come down against us? or who shall enter into our habitations?", insight:"Confidence in walls is confidence in straw; God walks through every defense."},
{q:"How would God punish them?", options:["According to the fruit of their doings","Beyond all measure","More lightly than they deserved","Only with exile"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:14",verse:"But I will punish you according to the fruit of your doings, saith the LORD: and I will kindle a fire in the forest thereof, and it shall devour all things round about it.", insight:"Fruit answers to seed; they would reap exactly what their deeds had planted."},
{q:"Where would God kindle a fire?", options:["In the forest thereof, to devour all things round about","In the temple only","In the king's palace","In the villages of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:14",verse:"But I will punish you according to the fruit of your doings, saith the LORD: and I will kindle a fire in the forest thereof, and it shall devour all things round about it.", insight:"The forest fed the city's pride; God burns the very source of their strength, and the fire circles everything."},
{q:"To whom was Jeremiah told to direct his answer?", options:["To Zedekiah","To Jehoiakim","To Jeconiah","To Gedaliah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 21:3",verse:"Then said Jeremiah unto them, Thus shall ye say to Zedekiah:", insight:"The word went straight to the throne; God's message does not dodge the powerful."}
]},
{n:22, name:"Justice in the King's House", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 22:16",verse:"He judged the cause of the poor and needy; then it was well with him: was not this to know me? saith the LORD."}, facts:["Did you know Shallum, the son of Josiah mourned in this chapter, is better known as Jehoahaz, who reigned only three months before Pharaoh carried him to Egypt (Jeremiah 22:11-12)?","Did you know Coniah in this chapter is the same king called Jeconiah elsewhere, and God said that even as His signet ring He would pluck him off (Jeremiah 22:24)?","Did you know the chapter ends with a triple cry, 'O earth, earth, earth, hear the word of the LORD,' before the sentence that no seed of Coniah would sit on David's throne (Jeremiah 22:29-30)?"], questions:[
{q:"Where was Jeremiah commanded to go and speak?", options:["Go down to the house of the king of Judah","Go up to the temple court","Go out to the valley of Hinnom","Go to the prison court"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:1",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; Go down to the house of the king of Judah, and speak there this word,", insight:"The word went down to the palace; God speaks where power sits."},
{q:"Who was told to hear the word of the LORD?", options:["O king of Judah, that sittest upon the throne of David","The queen mother of Judah","The captains of the host","The priests of the house"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:2",verse:"And say, Hear the word of the LORD, O king of Judah, that sittest upon the throne of David, thou, and thy servants, and thy people that enter in by these gates:", insight:"A throne of David carried covenant weight; privilege never excuses injustice."},
{q:"Which three helpless ones were named?", options:["The stranger, the fatherless, and the widow","The farmer, the merchant, and the soldier","The elder, the child, and the slave","The scribe, the priest, and the Levite"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:3",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; Execute ye judgment and righteousness, and deliver the spoiled out of the hand of the oppressor: and do no wrong, do no violence to the stranger, the fatherless, nor the widow, neither shed innocent blood in this place.", insight:"God names the ones the powerful forget; His justice is measured by the weakest."},
{q:"What were they forbidden to shed in that place?", options:["Innocent blood","The blood of sacrifices","The blood of their enemies","The blood of criminals"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:3",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; Execute ye judgment and righteousness, and deliver the spoiled out of the hand of the oppressor: and do no wrong, do no violence to the stranger, the fatherless, nor the widow, neither shed innocent blood in this place.", insight:"Innocent blood cries the loudest; a kingdom that sheds it signs its own sentence."},
{q:"If they did justice, who would enter the gates?", options:["Kings sitting upon the throne of David, riding in chariots and on horses","Priests bearing offerings","Merchants with treasure","Ambassadors from Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:4",verse:"For if ye do this thing indeed, then shall there enter in by the gates of this house kings sitting upon the throne of David, riding in chariots and on horses, he, and his servants, and his people.", insight:"Obedience keeps the dynasty; the promise of a throne hung on justice."},
{q:"By what did the LORD swear, if they would not hear?", options:["By myself","By the temple","By the covenant","By the angels"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:5",verse:"But if ye will not hear these words, I swear by myself, saith the LORD, that this house shall become a desolation.", insight:"God swearing by Himself leaves no higher appeal; His word is the last court."},
{q:"Though the king's house was as Gilead and Lebanon, what would God make it?", options:["A wilderness, and cities not inhabited","A fruitful garden","A royal storehouse","A high watchtower"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:6",verse:"For thus saith the LORD unto the king’s house of Judah; Thou art Gilead unto me, and the head of Lebanon: yet surely I will make thee a wilderness, and cities which are not inhabited.", insight:"The loveliest things become waste when God withdraws; beauty without obedience withers."},
{q:"What would the destroyers do with the choice cedars?", options:["Cut them down, and cast them into the fire","Carry them to Babylon","Build a new temple with them","Sell them to Tyre"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:7",verse:"And I will prepare destroyers against thee, every one with his weapons: and they shall cut down thy choice cedars, and cast them into the fire.", insight:"The cedars of pride make fine fuel; God burns what men boast in."},
{q:"What would the nations ask as they passed by the city?", options:["Wherefore hath the LORD done thus unto this great city?","Who built this desolation?","When shall it be rebuilt?","Who reigns here now?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:8",verse:"And many nations shall pass by this city, and they shall say every man to his neighbour, Wherefore hath the LORD done thus unto this great city?", insight:"Ruin preaches; the nations would read Jerusalem's story as a warning."},
{q:"What would the answer be?", options:["Because they have forsaken the covenant of the LORD their God, and worshipped other gods","Because Babylon was stronger","Because the walls were weak","Because the king was old"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:9",verse:"Then they shall answer, Because they have forsaken the covenant of the LORD their God, and worshipped other gods, and served them.", insight:"The verdict is covenant, not conquest; they fell from the inside first."},
{q:"For whom were they told to weep sore?", options:["For him that goeth away, for he shall return no more","For the dead king","For the burned temple","For the lost treasure"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:10",verse:"Weep ye not for the dead, neither bemoan him: but weep sore for him that goeth away: for he shall return no more, nor see his native country.", insight:"The living exile is worse than the honored dead; weep for the one who cannot come home."},
{q:"Who was Shallum?", options:["The son of Josiah, who reigned instead of his father","The son of David","A prophet of the LORD","A priest of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:11",verse:"For thus saith the LORD touching Shallum the son of Josiah king of Judah, which reigned instead of Josiah his father, which went forth out of this place; He shall not return thither any more:", insight:"Shallum, also called Jehoahaz, reigned only three months; short reigns can leave long sorrows."},
{q:"What would become of Shallum?", options:["He shall die in the place of his captivity, and see this land no more","He shall return in triumph","He shall reign forty years","He shall escape to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:12",verse:"But he shall die in the place whither they have led him captive, and shall see this land no more.", insight:"See this land no more is the bitterest exile; home lost is a wound that never closes."},
{q:"Against whom was the woe pronounced?", options:["Him that buildeth his house by unrighteousness","The poor of the land","The tillers of the soil","The keepers of the gate"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:13",verse:"Woe unto him that buildeth his house by unrighteousness, and his chambers by wrong; that useth his neighbour’s service without wages, and giveth him not for his work;", insight:"A wide house built on wronged labor is a monument to theft; God reads the payroll."},
{q:"What did the unjust builder do to his neighbor?", options:["Used his service without wages, and gave him not for his work","Stole his land","Burned his house","Sold his children"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:13",verse:"Woe unto him that buildeth his house by unrighteousness, and his chambers by wrong; that useth his neighbour’s service without wages, and giveth him not for his work;", insight:"Withholding wages is theft with a smile; God calls it unrighteousness."},
{q:"How did the builder describe his dream house?", options:["A wide house and large chambers, cieled with cedar, and painted with vermilion","A house of prayer for all people","A fortress of hewn stone","A tent of goats' hair"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:14",verse:"That saith, I will build me a wide house and large chambers, and cutteth him out windows; and it is cieled with cedar, and painted with vermilion.", insight:"Wide rooms, red paint, cedar ceilings; luxury bought with injustice is a painted tomb."},
{q:"What did Josiah do that made it well with him?", options:["He did judgment and justice","He built up the temple","He made war on Egypt","He stored up gold"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:15",verse:"Shalt thou reign, because thou closest thyself in cedar? did not thy father eat and drink, and do judgment and justice, and then it was well with him?", insight:"Josiah ate and drank and did justice; a good king is measured by righteousness, not riches."},
{q:"How did Josiah truly know the LORD?", options:["He judged the cause of the poor and needy","He offered many sacrifices","He wrote psalms","He fasted often"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:16",verse:"He judged the cause of the poor and needy; then it was well with him: was not this to know me? saith the LORD.", insight:"Was not this to know me? Knowing God is proven by defending the poor, not by religious display."},
{q:"What filled Jehoiakim's eyes and heart?", options:["Covetousness, shedding innocent blood, oppression, and violence","Wisdom and good counsel","Mercy and truth","The fear of the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:17",verse:"But thine eyes and thine heart are not but for thy covetousness, and for to shed innocent blood, and for oppression, and for violence, to do it.", insight:"Eyes and heart given to greed turn a king into a robber; what fills you will rule you."},
{q:"What burial awaited Jehoiakim?", options:["The burial of an ass, drawn and cast forth beyond the gates of Jerusalem","A royal tomb with his fathers","Burial in the house of the LORD","An honorable grave in the city"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 22:19",verse:"He shall be buried with the burial of an ass, drawn and cast forth beyond the gates of Jerusalem.", insight:"No lament, no honor; the king who lived for himself would be dragged out like a beast."}
]},
{n:23, name:"The Righteous Branch", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 23:5",verse:"Behold, the days come, saith the LORD, that I will raise unto David a righteous Branch, and a King shall reign and prosper, and shall execute judgment and justice in the earth."}, facts:["Did you know 'THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS' is one of the great names of God in Scripture, given here to the promised Branch from David (Jeremiah 23:5-6)?","Did you know the false prophets kept claiming 'I have dreamed, I have dreamed,' using dreams to make the people forget God's name (Jeremiah 23:25-27)?","Did you know God describes His own word as 'a fire' and 'a hammer that breaketh the rock in pieces,' the Bible's own picture of preaching that truly convicts (Jeremiah 23:29)?"], questions:[
{q:"Against whom is the woe pronounced?", options:["The pastors that destroy and scatter the sheep of my pasture","The merchants of the city","The soldiers of the guard","The tillers of the field"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:1",verse:"Woe be unto the pastors that destroy and scatter the sheep of my pasture! saith the LORD.", insight:"Shepherds who scatter are wolves in disguise; God holds leaders accountable for the flock."},
{q:"What had the pastors done to the flock?", options:["Scattered them, driven them away, and not visited them","Fed them in green pastures","Built strong folds for them","Led them beside still waters"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:2",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD God of Israel against the pastors that feed my people; Ye have scattered my flock, and driven them away, and have not visited them: behold, I will visit upon you the evil of your doings, saith the LORD.", insight:"Not visiting the sheep is itself a sin; neglect is a form of cruelty."},
{q:"What would God do to those pastors?", options:["Visit upon them the evil of their doings","Promote them to honor","Forget their deeds","Bless their houses"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:2",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD God of Israel against the pastors that feed my people; Ye have scattered my flock, and driven them away, and have not visited them: behold, I will visit upon you the evil of your doings, saith the LORD.", insight:"I will visit is God's promise to settle accounts; judgment begins with the shepherds."},
{q:"What would God do with the remnant of His flock?", options:["Gather them from all countries and bring them again to their folds","Leave them scattered forever","Give them to foreign shepherds","Scatter them even further"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:3",verse:"And I will gather the remnant of my flock out of all countries whither I have driven them, and will bring them again to their folds; and they shall be fruitful and increase.", insight:"The God who scattered in judgment gathers in mercy; His flock is never truly lost."},
{q:"What would the gathered flock do?", options:["Be fruitful and increase","Wander away again","Fight their shepherds","Build new cities"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:3",verse:"And I will gather the remnant of my flock out of all countries whither I have driven them, and will bring them again to their folds; and they shall be fruitful and increase.", insight:"Fruitfulness follows restoration; God's rescue always aims at flourishing."},
{q:"What shepherds would God set over them?", options:["Shepherds which shall feed them, so they fear no more","Shepherds hired from Egypt","Shepherds who tax the flock","Shepherds of the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:4",verse:"And I will set up shepherds over them which shall feed them: and they shall fear no more, nor be dismayed, neither shall they be lacking, saith the LORD.", insight:"Good shepherds feed, and fed sheep fear no more; security is the fruit of faithful feeding."},
{q:"What would God raise unto David?", options:["A righteous Branch","A cedar of Lebanon","A new temple","A golden crown"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:5",verse:"Behold, the days come, saith the LORD, that I will raise unto David a righteous Branch, and a King shall reign and prosper, and shall execute judgment and justice in the earth.", insight:"The Branch from David's line is Christ; from a cut down stump grows the King."},
{q:"What would the coming King do?", options:["Reign and prosper, and execute judgment and justice","Conquer the Romans","Rebuild Solomon's navy","Lay taxes on the nations"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:5",verse:"Behold, the days come, saith the LORD, that I will raise unto David a righteous Branch, and a King shall reign and prosper, and shall execute judgment and justice in the earth.", insight:"A King who reigns in justice; prosperity follows righteousness, not the other way round."},
{q:"By what name would He be called?", options:["THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS","The King of Salem","The Lion of the tribe","The Son of David"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:6",verse:"In his days Judah shall be saved, and Israel shall dwell safely: and this is his name whereby he shall be called, THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS.", insight:"His name is our hope: He is the righteousness we could never be."},
{q:"What would no longer be said?", options:["The LORD liveth, which brought up the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt","The LORD liveth, which gave the law at Sinai","Blessed be the name of the LORD","Holy, holy, holy is the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:7",verse:"Therefore, behold, the days come, saith the LORD, that they shall no more say, The LORD liveth, which brought up the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt;", insight:"A greater deliverance was coming, one that would outshine even the Exodus; God keeps raising the stakes of rescue."},
{q:"What would be said instead?", options:["The LORD liveth, which brought up the seed of Israel out of the north country","The LORD liveth, which destroyed Babylon","The LORD liveth, which made the heavens","The LORD liveth, which fed us in the desert"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:8",verse:"But, The LORD liveth, which brought up and which led the seed of the house of Israel out of the north country, and from all countries whither I had driven them; and they shall dwell in their own land.", insight:"The return from exile would become the new testimony; every generation gets its own proof of God."},
{q:"What was full of adulterers?", options:["The land","The temple court","The king's palace","The marketplace"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:10",verse:"For the land is full of adulterers; for because of swearing the land mourneth; the pleasant places of the wilderness are dried up, and their course is evil, and their force is not right.", insight:"When a land is full of adultery, the land itself mourns; sin spoils more than souls."},
{q:"Where had God found the prophets' and priests' wickedness?", options:["In my house","In the king's palace","In the land of Babylon","In the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:11",verse:"For both prophet and priest are profane; yea, in my house have I found their wickedness, saith the LORD.", insight:"Profaneness in God's own house is the deepest rot; corruption at the altar poisons everything."},
{q:"What would their way become?", options:["As slippery ways in the darkness","A broad and easy highway","A narrow, safe path","A pleasant meadow"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:12",verse:"Wherefore their way shall be unto them as slippery ways in the darkness: they shall be driven on, and fall therein: for I will bring evil upon them, even the year of their visitation, saith the LORD.", insight:"Slippery ways in darkness: those who lead others astray lose their own footing first."},
{q:"What had the prophets of Samaria done?", options:["Prophesied in Baal, and caused my people Israel to err","Prophesied the truth boldly","Refused to speak at all","Fled away to Judah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:13",verse:"And I have seen folly in the prophets of Samaria; they prophesied in Baal, and caused my people Israel to err.", insight:"Folly dressed as prophecy; a lie spoken for God is still a lie."},
{q:"What did the prophets of Jerusalem strengthen?", options:["The hands of evildoers","The hands of the poor","The walls of the city","The gates of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:14",verse:"I have seen also in the prophets of Jerusalem an horrible thing: they commit adultery, and walk in lies: they strengthen also the hands of evildoers, that none doth return from his wickedness: they are all of them unto me as Sodom, and the inhabitants thereof as Gomorrah.", insight:"Encouraging the wicked is the worst kind of preaching; flattery keeps sinners comfortable in sin."},
{q:"What would God feed the false prophets?", options:["Wormwood, and the water of gall","Manna from heaven","Milk and honey","The bread of life"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:15",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD of hosts concerning the prophets; Behold, I will feed them with wormwood, and make them drink the water of gall: for from the prophets of Jerusalem is profaneness gone forth into all the land.", insight:"Bitter food for bitter teachers; those who poisoned souls will taste bitterness themselves."},
{q:"What do the false prophets speak?", options:["A vision of their own heart, and not out of the mouth of the LORD","The exact word of the LORD","Nothing at all","Only silent prayers"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:16",verse:"Thus saith the LORD of hosts, Hearken not unto the words of the prophets that prophesy unto you: they make you vain: they speak a vision of their own heart, and not out of the mouth of the LORD.", insight:"Their own heart is the source of every false word; true prophecy starts with God's mouth, not man's imagination."},
{q:"What did they keep saying to those who despise the LORD?", options:["Ye shall have peace","Ye shall surely perish","Repent and be saved","Flee to the mountains"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:17",verse:"They say still unto them that despise me, The LORD hath said, Ye shall have peace; and they say unto every one that walketh after the imagination of his own heart, No evil shall come upon you.", insight:"Peace, peace to those who despise God is the devil's lullaby; comfort without repentance is cruelty."},
{q:"What is God's word like?", options:["A fire, and a hammer that breaketh the rock in pieces","A gentle breeze","A quiet whisper","A drop of dew"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 23:29",verse:"Is not my word like as a fire? saith the LORD; and like a hammer that breaketh the rock in pieces?", insight:"Fire and hammer: God's word burns the dross and shatters stone; it was never meant to tickle ears."}
]},
{n:24, name:"The Two Baskets of Figs", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 24:7",verse:"And I will give them an heart to know me, that I am the LORD: and they shall be my people, and I will be their God: for they shall return unto me with their whole heart."}, facts:["Did you know this vision came right after King Jeconiah was carried to Babylon, so the 'good figs' were the very captives people pitied (Jeremiah 24:1, 5)?","Did you know the KJV calls the bad figs 'very naughty,' an old English word meaning worthless or good for nothing (Jeremiah 24:2)?","Did you know God said He sent the captives to Babylon 'for their good,' turning exile itself into mercy (Jeremiah 24:5)?"], questions:[
{q:"What did the LORD show Jeremiah?", options:["Two baskets of figs set before the temple of the LORD","Two baskets of wheat at the gate","Two golden candlesticks","Two olive branches"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:1",verse:"The LORD shewed me, and, behold, two baskets of figs were set before the temple of the LORD, after that Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon had carried away captive Jeconiah the son of Jehoiakim king of Judah, and the princes of Judah, with the carpenters and smiths, from Jerusalem, and had brought them to Babylon.", insight:"A vision set before the temple, where God was supposed to dwell; He speaks where His people gather."},
{q:"When did this vision come?", options:["After Nebuchadrezzar carried away Jeconiah captive to Babylon","Before Josiah became king","During the wilderness years","After the return from exile"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:1",verse:"The LORD shewed me, and, behold, two baskets of figs were set before the temple of the LORD, after that Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon had carried away captive Jeconiah the son of Jehoiakim king of Judah, and the princes of Judah, with the carpenters and smiths, from Jerusalem, and had brought them to Babylon.", insight:"God's word arrives in the middle of disaster, not after it; He interprets the present."},
{q:"What was in the first basket?", options:["Very good figs, even like the figs that are first ripe","Rotten figs","Dried figs","Wild grapes"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:2",verse:"One basket had very good figs, even like the figs that are first ripe: and the other basket had very naughty figs, which could not be eaten, they were so bad.", insight:"First ripe figs are the choicest; even in captivity, God sees precious fruit."},
{q:"What was in the other basket?", options:["Very naughty figs, which could not be eaten, they were so bad","Sour, unripe figs","Figs mixed with thorns","Withered leaves"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:2",verse:"One basket had very good figs, even like the figs that are first ripe: and the other basket had very naughty figs, which could not be eaten, they were so bad.", insight:"Naughty in the old tongue means worthless; some fruit is so spoiled it cannot be salvaged."},
{q:"What did the LORD ask Jeremiah?", options:["What seest thou, Jeremiah?","Where art thou?","Whom shall I send?","Why weepest thou?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:3",verse:"Then said the LORD unto me, What seest thou, Jeremiah? And I said, Figs; the good figs, very good; and the evil, very evil, that cannot be eaten, they are so evil.", insight:"God asks what we see to open our eyes; the question is the beginning of understanding."},
{q:"How did Jeremiah answer?", options:["Figs; the good figs, very good; and the evil, very evil","Wheat and tares together","Sheep and goats","Gold and dross"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:3",verse:"Then said the LORD unto me, What seest thou, Jeremiah? And I said, Figs; the good figs, very good; and the evil, very evil, that cannot be eaten, they are so evil.", insight:"He simply described what he saw; honest seeing comes before true understanding."},
{q:"What came to Jeremiah again?", options:["The word of the LORD","A dream in the night","An angel","A letter from the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:4",verse:"Again the word of the LORD came unto me, saying,", insight:"God speaks again and again; when we miss it the first time, He repeats the mercy."},
{q:"What did the good figs represent?", options:["The captives of Judah, carried into the land of the Chaldeans","The remnant left in Jerusalem","The priests of the temple","The Egyptians"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:5",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, the God of Israel; Like these good figs, so will I acknowledge them that are carried away captive of Judah, whom I have sent out of this place into the land of the Chaldeans for their good.", insight:"The captives were the good figs; sometimes God's favor looks like exile."},
{q:"Why had God sent them into captivity?", options:["For their good","Only for punishment","To forget their homeland","To serve idols"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:5",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, the God of Israel; Like these good figs, so will I acknowledge them that are carried away captive of Judah, whom I have sent out of this place into the land of the Chaldeans for their good.", insight:"For their good: exile was medicine, not malice; God wounds to heal."},
{q:"What would God set upon the captives?", options:["His eyes, for good","His anger","A heavy yoke","Deep darkness"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:6",verse:"For I will set mine eyes upon them for good, and I will bring them again to this land: and I will build them, and not pull them down; and I will plant them, and not pluck them up.", insight:"His eyes upon them for good; even in Babylon, they were watched over, not watched."},
{q:"What would God do with them?", options:["Bring them again to this land","Leave them in Babylon forever","Scatter them to Egypt","Forget them utterly"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:6",verse:"For I will set mine eyes upon them for good, and I will bring them again to this land: and I will build them, and not pull them down; and I will plant them, and not pluck them up.", insight:"Again to this land: exile has a return ticket when God writes the itinerary."},
{q:"What heart would God give them?", options:["A heart to know me, that I am the LORD","A heart of stone","A fearful heart","A divided heart"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:7",verse:"And I will give them an heart to know me, that I am the LORD: and they shall be my people, and I will be their God: for they shall return unto me with their whole heart.", insight:"The greatest gift is not land but knowing; a new heart is worth more than a restored city."},
{q:"What covenant promise would be fulfilled?", options:["They shall be my people, and I will be their God","They shall be servants forever","They shall wander no more","They shall be kings"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:7",verse:"And I will give them an heart to know me, that I am the LORD: and they shall be my people, and I will be their God: for they shall return unto me with their whole heart.", insight:"My people, my God: the oldest promise, renewed in the darkest place."},
{q:"Whom did the evil figs represent?", options:["Zedekiah the king of Judah, his princes, and the residue of Jerusalem","The captives in Babylon","The priests","The prophets"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:8",verse:"And as the evil figs, which cannot be eaten, they are so evil; surely thus saith the LORD, So will I give Zedekiah the king of Judah, and his princes, and the residue of Jerusalem, that remain in this land, and them that dwell in the land of Egypt:", insight:"Those who stayed behind, clinging to the ruins, were the rotten fruit; remaining is not always faithfulness."},
{q:"Who else was counted with the evil figs?", options:["Them that dwell in the land of Egypt","The merchants of Tyre","The shepherds","The children"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:8",verse:"And as the evil figs, which cannot be eaten, they are so evil; surely thus saith the LORD, So will I give Zedekiah the king of Judah, and his princes, and the residue of Jerusalem, that remain in this land, and them that dwell in the land of Egypt:", insight:"Fleeing to Egypt looked like escape, but God counted them with the doomed; running from His discipline is no refuge."},
{q:"What would become of them?", options:["Delivered to be removed into all kingdoms for their hurt","Made princes in Babylon","Given peace in Egypt","Forgotten by all"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:9",verse:"And I will deliver them to be removed into all the kingdoms of the earth for their hurt, to be a reproach and a proverb, a taunt and a curse, in all places whither I shall drive them.", insight:"Removed for their hurt: there is a scattering that heals and a scattering that harms."},
{q:"What four things would they become?", options:["A reproach, a proverb, a taunt, and a curse","A blessing, a song, a joy, a crown","Rich, famous, honored, loved","Forgotten, hidden, silent, gone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:9",verse:"And I will deliver them to be removed into all the kingdoms of the earth for their hurt, to be a reproach and a proverb, a taunt and a curse, in all places whither I shall drive them.", insight:"Their name would become a warning on every lip; a wasted life preaches, too."},
{q:"What three judgments would God send among them?", options:["The sword, the famine, and the pestilence","Locusts, hail, and darkness","Lions, bears, and wolves","Fire, flood, and earthquake"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:10",verse:"And I will send the sword, the famine, and the pestilence, among them, till they be consumed from off the land that I gave unto them and to their fathers.", insight:"The familiar trio of judgment; God repeats the warning until it is heard."},
{q:"How long would these last?", options:["Till they be consumed from off the land","For seven days","For one year","Forever without end"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:10",verse:"And I will send the sword, the famine, and the pestilence, among them, till they be consumed from off the land that I gave unto them and to their fathers.", insight:"Till consumed: judgment finishes its work; God does not strike in vain."},
{q:"To whom did the word of the LORD come?", options:["Unto Jeremiah","Unto King Zedekiah","Unto the priests","Unto all the people"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 24:4",verse:"Again the word of the LORD came unto me, saying,", insight:"The word came to one man for a whole nation; God still entrusts His word to willing hearers."}
]},
{n:25, name:"Seventy Years of Captivity", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 25:11",verse:"And this whole land shall be a desolation, and an astonishment; and these nations shall serve the king of Babylon seventy years."}, facts:["Did you know the famous seventy years of captivity were first announced here, and Daniel was later found reading this very prophecy (Jeremiah 25:11-12)?","Did you know God calls the pagan Nebuchadrezzar 'my servant,' making him heaven's instrument for the hour (Jeremiah 25:9)?","Did you know Jeremiah was told to make all the nations drink 'the wine cup of this fury,' a list stretching from Jerusalem to Egypt to the kings of the north (Jeremiah 25:15-26)?"], questions:[
{q:"In whose reign did this word come?", options:["In the fourth year of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah","In the first year of Zedekiah","In Josiah's eighteenth year","In the tenth year of Jeconiah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:1",verse:"The word that came to Jeremiah concerning all the people of Judah in the fourth year of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah, that was the first year of Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon;", insight:"God's word is dated; He speaks into real years, not vague ages."},
{q:"What was that year in Babylon's reckoning?", options:["The first year of Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon","The tenth year of Nabopolassar","The last year of Belshazzar","The first year of Cyrus"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:1",verse:"The word that came to Jeremiah concerning all the people of Judah in the fourth year of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah, that was the first year of Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon;", insight:"Two kingdoms, two calendars, one God; heaven's clock runs over them both."},
{q:"How long had the word of the LORD been coming to Jeremiah?", options:["Three and twenty years, from the thirteenth year of Josiah","Seven years","Forty years","Twelve years"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:3",verse:"From the thirteenth year of Josiah the son of Amon king of Judah, even unto this day, that is the three and twentieth year, the word of the LORD hath come unto me, and I have spoken unto you, rising early and speaking; but ye have not hearkened.", insight:"Twenty-three years of preaching with little to show; faithfulness is not measured by applause."},
{q:"How does Jeremiah describe his preaching?", options:["Rising early and speaking","Speaking only on the Sabbath","Writing letters to the king","Singing in the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:3",verse:"From the thirteenth year of Josiah the son of Amon king of Judah, even unto this day, that is the three and twentieth year, the word of the LORD hath come unto me, and I have spoken unto you, rising early and speaking; but ye have not hearkened.", insight:"Rising early is diligence; God's messengers do not sleep in while souls perish."},
{q:"What had the LORD done with His prophets?", options:["Sent them, rising early and sending them","Kept them silent","Sent only Jeremiah","Hidden them in caves"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:4",verse:"And the LORD hath sent unto you all his servants the prophets, rising early and sending them; but ye have not hearkened, nor inclined your ear to hear.", insight:"God rises early too; heaven is never late in warning."},
{q:"What did the prophets say?", options:["Turn ye again now every one from his evil way","Pay more tribute to the king","Rebuild the temple walls","Flee to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:5",verse:"They said, Turn ye again now every one from his evil way, and from the evil of your doings, and dwell in the land that the LORD hath given unto you and to your fathers for ever and ever:", insight:"The message never changes: turn. Repentance is still the first word of God."},
{q:"What were they not to go after?", options:["Other gods, to serve and worship them","Other lands to conquer","Other wives","Other trades"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:6",verse:"And go not after other gods to serve them, and to worship them, and provoke me not to anger with the works of your hands; and I will do you no hurt.", insight:"Going after other gods is adultery of the soul; God will not share His people."},
{q:"What startling name does God give Nebuchadrezzar?", options:["My servant","My enemy","My shepherd","My anointed"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:9",verse:"Behold, I will send and take all the families of the north, saith the LORD, and Nebuchadrezzar the king of Babylon, my servant, and will bring them against this land, and against the inhabitants thereof, and against all these nations round about, and will utterly destroy them, and make them an astonishment, and an hissing, and perpetual desolations.", insight:"Even a pagan conqueror can be God's servant; heaven drafts whom it will for its purposes."},
{q:"From where would God take the families of judgment?", options:["All the families of the north","The families of the south","The families of Egypt","The families of the sea coast"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:9",verse:"Behold, I will send and take all the families of the north, saith the LORD, and Nebuchadrezzar the king of Babylon, my servant, and will bring them against this land, and against the inhabitants thereof, and against all these nations round about, and will utterly destroy them, and make them an astonishment, and an hissing, and perpetual desolations.", insight:"Judgment comes from an unexpected direction; God's armies march where He points."},
{q:"What joyful voices would God take away?", options:["The voice of mirth, the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride","The voice of the prophets","The voice of the priests","The voice of the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:10",verse:"Moreover I will take from them the voice of mirth, and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride, the sound of the millstones, and the light of the candle.", insight:"No weddings, no laughter; when God silences joy, the silence preaches."},
{q:"What everyday sounds would cease?", options:["The sound of the millstones, and the light of the candle","The bleating of the sheep","The hammering of the smiths","The crowing of the roosters"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:10",verse:"Moreover I will take from them the voice of mirth, and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride, the sound of the millstones, and the light of the candle.", insight:"The millstone's song and the candle's glow are daily mercies; losing the ordinary is judgment's deepest cut."},
{q:"How long would these nations serve the king of Babylon?", options:["Seventy years","Forty years","Seven years","A hundred years"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:11",verse:"And this whole land shall be a desolation, and an astonishment; and these nations shall serve the king of Babylon seventy years.", insight:"Seventy years, a set number; even judgment has a limit when God sets it."},
{q:"What would happen when the seventy years were accomplished?", options:["God would punish the king of Babylon for his iniquity","Babylon would rule forever","Israel would forget the LORD","The temple would fall"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:12",verse:"And it shall come to pass, when seventy years are accomplished, that I will punish the king of Babylon, and that nation, saith the LORD, for their iniquity, and the land of the Chaldeans, and will make it perpetual desolations.", insight:"The rod gets broken after it is used; God judges the judge."},
{q:"What would the land of the Chaldeans become?", options:["Perpetual desolations","A greater empire","A holy city","A trading port"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:12",verse:"And it shall come to pass, when seventy years are accomplished, that I will punish the king of Babylon, and that nation, saith the LORD, for their iniquity, and the land of the Chaldeans, and will make it perpetual desolations.", insight:"Perpetual is a long word; what God finishes stays finished."},
{q:"What cup did the LORD put in Jeremiah's hand?", options:["The wine cup of this fury","The cup of blessing","The cup of cold water","A golden cup"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:15",verse:"For thus saith the LORD God of Israel unto me; Take the wine cup of this fury at my hand, and cause all the nations, to whom I send thee, to drink it.", insight:"Fury has a cup, and God pours it Himself; His wrath is measured, not wild."},
{q:"What would the nations do after drinking?", options:["Drink, and be moved, and be mad, because of the sword","Rejoice and sing","Sleep in peace","Grow strong"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:16",verse:"And they shall drink, and be moved, and be mad, because of the sword that I will send among them.", insight:"Drinking God's wrath maddens; sin's cup never satisfies, it only staggers."},
{q:"Who was made to drink first?", options:["Jerusalem, and the cities of Judah, and the kings and princes thereof","Pharaoh and Egypt","Babylon","Edom and Moab"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:18",verse:"To wit, Jerusalem, and the cities of Judah, and the kings thereof, and the princes thereof, to make them a desolation, an astonishment, an hissing, and a curse; as it is this day;", insight:"Judgment begins at the house of God; Jerusalem drinks first because she knew the most."},
{q:"Who is named right after Judah?", options:["Pharaoh king of Egypt, his servants, his princes, and all his people","The king of Assyria and his army","The queen of Sheba","The sailors of Tarshish"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:19",verse:"Pharaoh king of Egypt, and his servants, and his princes, and all his people;", insight:"Egypt, Judah's false hope, drinks next; every refuge we trust instead of God gets judged."},
{q:"What would the LORD do from on high?", options:["Roar, and give a shout as they that tread the grapes","Whisper softly","Remain silent","Sing a lullaby"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:30",verse:"Therefore prophesy thou against them all these words, and say unto them, The LORD shall roar from on high, and utter his voice from his holy habitation; he shall mightily roar upon his habitation; he shall give a shout, as they that tread the grapes, against all the inhabitants of the earth.", insight:"God roars like the harvest shout at the winepress; His judgment is as certain as the vintage."},
{q:"How far would the slain of the LORD stretch?", options:["From one end of the earth even unto the other end","From Jerusalem to Babylon","From Egypt to Assyria","From Dan to Beersheba"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 25:33",verse:"And the slain of the LORD shall be at that day from one end of the earth even unto the other end of the earth: they shall not be lamented, neither gathered, nor buried; they shall be dung upon the ground.", insight:"No borders contain it; when God judges the nations, the whole earth is the field."}
]}
]);
